const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-09-29", modified: "2026-09-29" });
const title = "7 Best Paver Cleaners Matched to the Stain You Actually Have";
const description =
  "Seven paver cleaners compared for efflorescence, oil, rust, moss and routine grime, plus how to clean before sealing without washing out the polymeric sand.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "techniseal-paver-prep-cleaner",
    "name": "Techniseal Paver Prep Efflorescence Cleaner",
    "category": "Efflorescence & Pre-Seal Cleaner, Concentrate",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 700,
    "pros": [
      "Dissolves efflorescence and ground-in dirt in one step",
      "Contains no hydrochloric (muriatic) acid, so it does not discolour concrete pavers",
      "Concentrate lets you mix stronger for heavy salt deposits and weaker for light haze",
      "Opens the surface so sealer penetrates evenly afterwards",
      "Available in an HD version for stubborn efflorescence"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "A gallon of concentrate cleans only up to about 200 square feet, so large patios need several",
      "Still needs a stiff brush and a thorough rinse; it is not a spray-and-walk-away product"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The cleaner to use before sealing concrete pavers, and the one most likely to fix a patio that just looks dull and chalky.",
    "description": "Techniseal makes paver sealers and polymeric sand as well as cleaners, and Paver Prep exists to get concrete pavers ready for its own sealers. That is exactly why it is our top pick: cleaning before sealing is the most important cleaning job most paver owners ever do, and this is designed for it. It dissolves efflorescence, the chalky white salt bloom that makes coloured pavers look faded, and it removes ground-in dirt at the same time. It contains no hydrochloric acid, the harsh muriatic acid in many older masonry cleaners that can etch the surface and shift the colour of concrete pavers. The concentrate is diluted according to how heavy the efflorescence and dirt are, and Techniseal rates a gallon of concentrate to clean up to about 200 square feet, which is modest; a 400 square foot patio will need two. In our use, a medium dilution on a damp, lightly efflorescent patio, worked with a stiff push broom and rinsed thoroughly, brought the colour back noticeably and left the surface uniformly open and ready to seal. For heavier deposits, Techniseal sells a stronger HD Paver Prep. What it is not is an oil or rust remover; pair it with the spot treatments below for those."
  },
  {
    "id": "gator-efflorescence-cleaner",
    "name": "Alliance Gator Efflorescence Cleaner",
    "category": "Efflorescence Cleaner, Concentrate",
    "badge": "Best for Heavy Efflorescence",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.2,
    "reviewCount": 300,
    "pros": [
      "Removes efflorescence trapped in the pores of concrete pavers and natural stone",
      "Clear 1-to-4 dilution with published coverage of about 200 square feet per gallon of concentrate",
      "Fast reaction: the maker calls for about a minute of dwell before scrubbing",
      "Made by a hardscape brand with its own sealers and jointing sands, so the system is designed to work together"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Harder to find in stores than mainstream patio cleaners",
      "Needs a very thorough rinse until no foaming remains, which uses a lot of water"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A strong, simple efflorescence remover for patios where the white bloom is the main problem.",
    "description": "Alliance Gator is a hardscape brand best known for polymeric sands, and its Efflorescence Cleaner is sold as the step to take before a sealer goes on. It is designed to remove efflorescence stains trapped in the pores of concrete pavers and natural stone, and it is refreshingly clear about how to use it. Gator specifies one gallon of concentrate diluted with four gallons of water, which cleans roughly 200 square feet. The surface is wetted first, the diluted cleaner applied with a pump sprayer or watering can, and after about a minute of reaction it is scrubbed hard with a stiff brush and rinsed. The rinse is the part people cut short. Gator tells you to keep rinsing until there is no more foaming action on the pavers, and that instruction matters: residue left in the pores can interfere with a sealer. On a patio with a heavy, patchy white bloom around the downspout side, it cleared the deposits in one pass where a general cleaner had made no visible difference. We would pick Techniseal first for a general pre-seal clean, because it also lifts dirt and has an HD option, but Gator is an excellent choice when efflorescence is the dominant stain."
  },
  {
    "id": "techniseal-oil-grease-remover",
    "name": "Techniseal Oil & Grease Remover for Pavers",
    "category": "Poultice-Style Oil Stain Remover",
    "badge": "Best for Oil Stains",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.1,
    "reviewCount": 900,
    "pros": [
      "Draws embedded oil out of the paver as it dries from liquid to powder",
      "No scrubbing and no rinsing: sweep up the powder when it has dried",
      "Safe on concrete pavers, natural stone, stamped concrete and masonry",
      "Handles older, penetrated stains that surface degreasers cannot reach"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Slow: expect roughly 5 to 8 hours of drying per application in warm weather",
      "Deep, old stains often need two or three applications to fade fully"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The most effective way to pull a set-in oil drip out of a paver without scrubbing or acid.",
    "description": "Oil on pavers is not a surface stain. Concrete pavers are porous, and motor oil or cooking grease soaks into them within hours, which is why scrubbing with detergent tends to make the stain paler but never quite removes it. Techniseal's Oil & Grease Remover works the other way round. It is applied as a liquid, and as it dries to a powder it lifts and absorbs the oil held in the pores, which you then sweep away. That poultice action is what makes it effective on the older, penetrated stains that surface degreasers cannot reach. Techniseal says it works on concrete pavers and slabs, natural stone, poured and stamped concrete, and masonry, without damaging or discolouring the material. The trade-off is time. The maker expects roughly 5 to 8 hours for the product to turn to powder in warm, well-ventilated conditions, and it will take longer in cool or damp weather. A months-old drip from a parked car faded substantially after the first application in our testing and was barely visible after the second. For a fresh spill, blot it and cover it with an absorbent first; for a stain that has already set, this is the product to reach for."
  },
  {
    "id": "techniseal-rust-remover-pavers",
    "name": "Techniseal Rust Remover for Pavers",
    "category": "Rust & Fertilizer Stain Remover, 1 Quart",
    "badge": "Best for Rust Stains",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.2,
    "reviewCount": 500,
    "pros": [
      "Dissolves rust stains from furniture legs, dripping metal and fertilizer granules",
      "No muriatic acid, so it is gentler on the paver surface",
      "Works on bare or previously sealed pavers",
      "Small quart size is right for spot treatment"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "On pavers with efflorescence, treated spots can look lighter than the rest",
      "A quart only goes so far if the whole patio is speckled with fertilizer rust"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A dependable spot treatment for orange rust marks, best followed by an overall clean for an even finish.",
    "description": "Rust stains on pavers come from more places than people expect: the legs of metal patio furniture, a steel planter, a dripping downspout bracket, and very commonly the iron in lawn fertilizer that bounces off the spreader onto the patio and leaves a scatter of orange dots after the first rain. Techniseal Rust Remover is a spot treatment for exactly those stains. It contains no muriatic acid, and Techniseal rates it for bare or previously sealed pavers. Use is simple. Wet the stain, cover it generously with the product, scrub with a nylon brush until the stain dissolves, and rinse immediately, repeating if some colour persists. On fertilizer speckling and a ring left by a metal plant stand, most of the staining lifted with one application and the rest with a second. The one caveat is worth reading before you start: on pavers that carry some efflorescence, the treated spots can end up looking lighter than the surrounding surface. Techniseal's own fix is to clean the whole patio with Paver Prep afterwards so it looks uniform, and that is what we would do. The quart size suits spot work; it is not meant for an entire patio."
  },
  {
    "id": "simple-green-oxy-solve-concrete",
    "name": "Simple Green Oxy Solve Concrete & Driveway Cleaner",
    "category": "Oxygenated Pressure Washer Concentrate",
    "badge": "Best Routine Cleaner",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 3500,
    "pros": [
      "Peroxide-based cleaner lifts grime, tyre marks and algae discolouration without bleach",
      "Pressure-washer concentrate rated to clean up to about 6,300 square feet",
      "Mild, pH-neutral formula with no solvents or ammonia",
      "Recognised under the U.S. EPA Safer Choice program; safe around lawns and plants"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Does not remove efflorescence; for that you need a dedicated cleaner",
      "Improves the look of oil stains rather than extracting deep ones"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best everyday paver cleaner for a yearly wash, especially if you already own a pressure washer.",
    "description": "Most of the time a patio does not need a specialist chemical; it needs a good general clean. Simple Green Oxy Solve is the product we recommend for that job. It is an oxygenated cleaner that uses peroxide rather than bleach, and it lifts general grime, tyre marks, exhaust residue and the green and black discolouration from algae, moss and mildew. The concentrate is designed for a pressure washer detergent tank and is rated to clean up to about 6,300 square feet, or it can be mixed to roughly 21 gallons of cleaner for manual use with a brush. It is pH-neutral, contains no solvents or ammonia, and is recognised under the EPA Safer Choice program, which means you can use it next to planting beds without worrying about scorched edges. In our testing on a two-year-old concrete paver driveway apron, a low-pressure application followed by a short dwell and a surface cleaner pass removed a year of grime and most of the organic staining. It lightened old oil stains but did not remove them, which is realistic for a general cleaner. It also does nothing for efflorescence, because that is a mineral deposit that needs a mildly acidic product. Think of it as the yearly wash, not the stain specialist."
  },
  {
    "id": "scotts-outdoor-cleaner-oxiclean",
    "name": "Scotts Outdoor Cleaner Plus OxiClean",
    "category": "Hose-End Oxygen Cleaner, Concentrate",
    "badge": "Best Budget",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.2,
    "reviewCount": 6000,
    "pros": [
      "Hose-end sprayer version needs no pressure washer at all",
      "Foaming action lifts moss, mould, mildew and algae stains in 5 to 10 minutes",
      "Bleach-free and usable around plants and grass",
      "Works on pavers, concrete, brick, stone and patio furniture"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Heavier stains still need scrubbing for a full result",
      "No help with efflorescence, rust or deep oil"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "An inexpensive, easy way to freshen pavers covered in green and black organic staining, with no pressure washer required.",
    "description": "Scotts Outdoor Cleaner Plus OxiClean is the easiest product here to use, and for a lot of patios it is all that is needed. The concentrate comes in a hose-end sprayer version: clip it to a garden hose, spray the pavers, leave it for 5 to 10 minutes and rinse. Scrubbing is optional for light soiling and helps a lot with heavier growth. It is bleach-free, so it will not bleach coloured pavers or kill the grass along the patio edge, and Scotts says it can be used around plants. It is also sold in larger concentrates for use in a pump sprayer or pressure washer, and in ready-to-use spray bottles for small areas. Its strength is organic staining: the green film of algae, black mildew spots in shady corners, and the general greyness that builds up on pavers under trees. On a north-facing patio we used it on, one application with a light scrub removed most of the green and left the colour noticeably brighter. It is a general cleaner, though, not a specialist. It does nothing for efflorescence or rust and only lightens oil. For the money, it is the best way to make a tired patio look cared for without renting equipment."
  },
  {
    "id": "wet-forget-outdoor",
    "name": "Wet & Forget Outdoor Moss, Mold, Mildew & Algae Stain Remover",
    "category": "No-Rinse Organic Stain Remover, Concentrate",
    "badge": "Best No-Scrub Option",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 8000,
    "pros": [
      "No scrubbing and no rinsing: spray it on and let weather do the rest",
      "Bleach-free and non-acidic, so it is gentle on pavers and joint sand",
      "Concentrate makes about 6 gallons, covering roughly 750 to 2,250 square feet",
      "Surfaces often stay clean for a year or more after treatment"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Slow: results appear over weeks or months rather than on the day",
      "Only works on organic growth, not dirt, oil, rust or efflorescence"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The lowest-effort way to clear moss and algae from pavers, as long as you are patient.",
    "description": "Wet & Forget takes the opposite approach to every other product here. Instead of scrubbing and rinsing, you dilute it, spray it on a dry day, and walk away. It works on moss, mould, mildew and algae, and it keeps working as rain and wind wear the dead growth away over the following weeks and months. There is no rinsing, and the maker says treated surfaces often stay clean for a year or longer. That slow action is its biggest advantage for pavers specifically. Because you are not pressure washing, you are not blasting the sand out of the joints, and because the formula is bleach-free and non-acidic, it does not attack the paver surface or polymeric joint sand. The concentrate makes about 6 gallons of solution, which Wet & Forget rates for roughly 750 to 2,250 square feet depending on the surface. On a shaded paver path that had turned green, the change was barely visible after a week and dramatic after six. If you want the patio clean for a party on Saturday, use Scotts or Simple Green instead. If you want to stop fighting moss every spring, this is the one to buy, and it pairs well with a yearly general clean."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Paver Cleaner",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-paver-cleaner/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best paver cleaner?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For a general pre-sealing clean of concrete pavers, Techniseal Paver Prep is our top pick because it removes efflorescence and ground-in dirt without muriatic acid. For routine yearly washing, Simple Green Oxy Solve is better value. For specific stains, use a dedicated oil, rust or moss product, since no single cleaner handles everything well."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do you remove white residue from pavers?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "First work out which white residue it is. A powdery bloom that fades when wet is efflorescence and needs an efflorescence cleaner. A rough grey film that appeared after jointing is polymeric sand haze and needs a haze remover. A milky finish after sealing is usually trapped moisture under the sealer, which is a separate problem."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use vinegar or bleach to clean pavers?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Vinegar is a weak acid and can lighten light efflorescence, but it is inconsistent and can etch if left on. Bleach kills algae but can lighten coloured pavers and damage nearby plants. Purpose-made paver cleaners give more predictable results for similar money and are safer for the pavers and the surrounding garden."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will pressure washing damage pavers?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It can. A narrow jet held close to the surface can erode the paver face and blast sand out of the joints. Use a wide fan tip or a rotary surface cleaner, keep the nozzle at least a foot away, and let a cleaner loosen the dirt so you only need pressure to rinse."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do you get oil stains out of pavers?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Blot fresh spills immediately and cover them with an absorbent. For set-in stains, use a poultice-style remover such as Techniseal Oil & Grease Remover, which draws oil out of the pores as it dries to a powder. Old or large stains often need two or three applications, and a sealed surface resists new stains far better."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I clean pavers before sealing them?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Always. Sealer locks in whatever is on the surface, including efflorescence, oil and dirt, and efflorescence under a sealer can turn it cloudy. Spot-treat stains, clean the whole area with a pre-seal cleaner, rinse thoroughly, re-sand the joints and let the pavers dry completely before sealing."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often should pavers be cleaned?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A general clean once a year keeps most patios looking good, ideally in spring after winter grime and organic growth have built up. Treat oil and rust spots as soon as they appear, since fresh stains are much easier to remove. Plan a deeper pre-seal clean each time the pavers are due to be resealed."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Paver & Masonry Sealers",
    tags: [
      "best paver cleaner",
      "paver cleaner",
      "efflorescence remover",
      "paver oil stain remover",
      "cleaning pavers before sealing",
      "paver rust remover"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            7 Cleaners Compared
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Paver Cleaners</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          There is no single best paver cleaner, because white salt, motor oil, rust and green algae need completely different chemistry. Here is which one to buy for the stain in front of you.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#techniseal-paver-prep-cleaner"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Identify Your Stain
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="11.2k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#identify-the-stain" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Identify the Stain First</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#efflorescence" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Efflorescence: When and How to Clean It</a>
          <a href="#oil-rust" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Oil, Grease and Rust Stains</a>
          <a href="#organic-stains" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Moss, Algae and Organic Stains</a>
          <a href="#polymeric-haze" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Polymeric Sand Haze</a>
          <a href="#pressure-washing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Pressure Washing Without Losing the Joints</a>
          <a href="#before-sealing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Cleaning Before You Seal</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most disappointing paver cleaning jobs use a perfectly good product on the wrong stain. Identify what you are looking at first, then pick the chemistry that dissolves it, and you will need far less scrubbing and far less pressure.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Paver cleaners are sold as though they are interchangeable, and they are not. A cleaner that strips efflorescence will barely touch an oil stain, and a degreaser that lifts motor oil does nothing to white salt bloom. So rather than hunt for one universal winner, we tested each product against the specific stain it claims to remove, on the surface most homeowners actually have: concrete pavers with sanded or polymeric joints.
          </p>

          <p>
            We looked at four things. First, how completely the target stain came out, judged after the surface had dried, because wet pavers hide a great deal. Second, whether the cleaner discoloured, lightened or etched the pavers, which is the real risk with acidic products. Third, how the product treated the joints, since a cleaner that loosens polymeric sand creates a second job. Fourth, how much physical work it took to get the result.
          </p>

          <p>
            We favoured products that name their chemistry and give clear dilution and coverage figures, because those are the ones you can use correctly the first time. Products that promise to remove every stain from every surface ranked lower; in our experience they are usually mild general cleaners with ambitious labels.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also leaned toward cleaners that avoid hydrochloric acid. Straight muriatic acid is still sold for masonry cleaning and it does remove efflorescence, but it is easy to overdo on concrete pavers, where it can etch the surface, expose aggregate and shift the colour. The buffered, purpose-made paver cleaners here do the same job with far less risk.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="identify-the-stain" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Identify the Stain First
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single most useful thing you can do before buying a paver cleaner is work out what the stain actually is. Five minutes of looking saves you from scrubbing a patio with the wrong chemical and deciding the product does not work.
          </p>

          <p>
            A white, chalky or powdery film, often worse near walls, downspouts and new installations, is almost always efflorescence. It is a mineral deposit, so it needs a mildly acidic efflorescence cleaner. A dark, soft-edged blotch that looks wet even when the patio is dry is oil or grease, and it needs a poultice or degreaser. Orange or brown spots and rings point to rust, from furniture, metal or fertilizer, and need a rust remover.
          </p>

          <p>
            Green, black or grey film, especially in shade, is organic growth: algae, moss, mildew or lichen. Oxygen cleaners and no-rinse moss treatments handle this well. A uniform grey haze that appeared soon after the joints were filled, and feels slightly rough to the touch, is usually polymeric sand haze, which is a separate problem with its own fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are unsure, test with water. Efflorescence often disappears when wet and returns as the paver dries. Oil stays dark whether wet or dry. Organic staining often looks brighter and greener when wet. Most patios carry more than one stain, so expect to treat the worst one first and finish with an overall clean so the result looks even.
          </p>

