        <div id="efflorescence" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Efflorescence: When and How to Clean It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Efflorescence is the white salt deposit that appears on concrete pavers, especially in the first year or two after installation. Water moving through the paver carries soluble minerals to the surface, the water evaporates, and the minerals are left behind as a chalky bloom. It does not damage the paver, but it dulls the colour badly and it must be removed before sealing, or the sealer will lock it in.
          </p>

          <p>
            The key question is timing. On a new installation, efflorescence often keeps appearing for months as the pavers work through the minerals in them. Cleaning it off after the first bloom can be wasted effort if more is coming. Many installers recommend waiting until the pavers have been through a season of weather and the bloom has slowed before doing a thorough clean and seal.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you do clean, wet the pavers first so the cleaner reacts at the surface rather than soaking deep into dry pores. Apply the diluted efflorescence cleaner, give it the short dwell the label calls for, scrub with a stiff brush, and rinse far more than you think necessary. Work in manageable sections so the cleaner never dries on the surface, which is what causes blotchy results.
          </p>

          <p>
            If white marks appear after sealing rather than before, they may not be efflorescence at all. Moisture trapped under a sealer can turn it cloudy, and that needs a different fix. Our guide to the <a href="/best-paver-sealer/">best paver sealer</a> covers which formulas breathe well enough to reduce that risk.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="oil-rust" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Oil, Grease and Rust Stains
          </h2>

          <p>
            Oil and rust are the two stains most likely to survive a general cleaning, because both penetrate the paver rather than sitting on top of it. The good news is that both respond well to the right spot treatment, and neither requires aggressive chemistry if you are patient.
          </p>

          <p>
            For oil, speed matters. A fresh spill should be blotted and covered with an absorbent such as clay litter straight away, then swept up; the less oil soaks in, the easier the job. For a stain that has already set, a poultice-style remover such as Techniseal Oil & Grease Remover is the most effective approach, because it pulls the oil back out of the pores as it dries rather than trying to scrub it off the surface. Expect deep stains to need more than one application. Our guide to the <a href="/best-oil-stain-remover-for-concrete/">best oil stain removers for concrete</a> covers stronger options for poured slabs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rust is different chemistry: it is iron oxide, and it needs an acidic rust remover to dissolve it. Treat the spots individually with a brush, rinse immediately, and repeat on stubborn marks rather than leaving the product to sit longer than the label says. Because rust removers are acidic, treated spots can look slightly lighter than untreated paver around them, which is why it pays to finish with an overall clean.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prevention is easier than removal for both. Put a drip mat under a car that leaks, lift metal furniture onto plastic feet, and blow or sweep fertilizer granules off the patio before the first watering. Sealed pavers also resist oil and rust penetration far better than bare ones, which is one of the strongest arguments for sealing a driveway made of pavers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="organic-stains" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Moss, Algae and Organic Stains
          </h2>

          <p>
            Green and black staining is the most common problem on older paver patios, especially in shade, under trees, and anywhere water drains slowly. It is also the easiest to remove, because algae, moss and mildew are living growth that oxygen cleaners and moss treatments kill readily.
          </p>

          <p>
            You have two approaches. Fast-acting oxygen cleaners, such as Scotts Outdoor Cleaner Plus OxiClean or Simple Green Oxy Solve, give a visible result the same day with a short dwell and a scrub or low-pressure rinse. No-rinse treatments such as Wet & Forget work slowly over weeks, with no scrubbing at all, and tend to keep the surface clean for longer.
          </p>

          <p>
            We generally avoid chlorine bleach on pavers. It does kill growth, but it can lighten coloured concrete, it scorches plants and grass at the edges, and it offers no lasting protection. The oxygen-based and quaternary products here do the job without those drawbacks.
          </p>

          <p>
            If growth keeps coming back quickly, fix the cause. Standing water, overhanging branches and poor drainage feed algae, and joints that have lost their sand give moss a place to root. Re-sanding joints with a good polymeric sand, covered in our <a href="/best-polymeric-sand/">best polymeric sand</a> guide, removes that foothold and makes the next cleaning much easier.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="polymeric-haze" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Polymeric Sand Haze
          </h2>

          <p>
            Polymeric sand haze is a thin grey or white film left on the paver surface when the polymer binder in jointing sand hardens on the face of the paver instead of only in the joint. It usually appears within days of installation or re-sanding, it feels slightly rough, and ordinary paver cleaners will not shift it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cause is almost always installation technique: sand dust left on the surface before wetting, too much water flooding the sand across the paver face, or wetting the joints while the pavers were hot or damp. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-use-polymeric-sand/">how to use polymeric sand</a> walks through the sweeping, blowing and misting steps that prevent it in the first place.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once haze has formed, it needs a dedicated haze or slurry remover designed to break down the cured polymer without attacking the joint sand. Several hardscape brands, including Techniseal and Alliance Gator, sell these alongside their jointing sands. General detergents, oxygen cleaners and efflorescence cleaners are the wrong tools, and scrubbing hard with them just scratches the surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not seal over haze. A sealer will lock it in and make it more visible, not less. Remove it first, rinse, let the pavers dry fully, then seal. Our step-by-step guide on <a href="/how-to-remove-polymeric-sand-haze-from-pavers/">how to remove polymeric sand haze from pavers</a> covers the process and the products in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pressure-washing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Pressure Washing Without Losing the Joints
          </h2>

          <p>
            A pressure washer is the fastest way to rinse cleaner off a patio and the fastest way to ruin the joints. Pavers depend on the sand between them to lock together, and a narrow high-pressure jet held close to a joint will blast that sand out in seconds, whether it is regular or polymeric.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fix is technique rather than a weaker machine. Let the cleaner do the work, so you only need pressure to rinse. Use a wide fan tip, keep the nozzle at least a foot from the surface, and aim at a shallow angle across the pavers rather than straight down into the joints. A rotary surface cleaner attachment is even better, because it keeps the spray at a fixed height and spreads the force evenly across the face of the pavers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Accept that some joint sand will be lost on older installations, and plan to top it up. If you are going to seal, cleaning is the natural time to re-sand the joints, and polymeric sand is the usual choice for patios and driveways. Let everything dry thoroughly before sanding, because polymeric sand poured into damp joints can activate early and haze the surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pressure is not a substitute for chemistry on the stubborn stains. Oil, rust and efflorescence sit in the pores, and blasting them hard enough to shift them will usually damage the paver surface first. Treat those stains with the right product, then use the pressure washer only to rinse.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="before-sealing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Cleaning Before You Seal
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealing locks in whatever is on the paver surface, good or bad. Efflorescence, oil, rust, organic film and haze all become harder to remove once a sealer covers them, and some, efflorescence especially, can turn the new sealer cloudy. A thorough clean is therefore not an optional extra before sealing; it is the step that decides whether the sealer looks good.
          </p>

          <p>
            The order that works is: spot-treat oil and rust first, then do an overall clean with an efflorescence and pre-seal cleaner such as Techniseal Paver Prep, rinse very thoroughly, and re-sand the joints once the pavers are dry. Our guide on <a href="/how-long-after-polymeric-sand-can-you-seal-pavers/">how long after polymeric sand you can seal pavers</a> covers the waiting time between jointing and sealing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Drying time is where most sealing jobs go wrong. Pavers need to be fully dry, not just dry on the surface, before sealer goes on, and after a heavy rinse that usually means at least a day or two of dry weather. Moisture trapped under the sealer is the most common cause of a milky white finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once the surface is clean, dry and re-sanded, follow our guide on <a href="/how-to-apply-paver-sealer/">how to apply paver sealer</a> for coverage, coats and timing. If your project also involves an asphalt drive next to the pavers, fix any potholes there before you clean and seal, so patch material and dust do not end up on fresh sealer; our guide to the <a href="/best-asphalt-patch/">best asphalt patch</a> covers which products to use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using one cleaner for every stain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Efflorescence, oil, rust and algae need different chemistry. A general cleaner will lighten some of them and leave the rest untouched, and the usual response is to scrub harder or add pressure. Identify the stain first, spot-treat it with the right product, then do one overall clean for an even finish.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reaching for straight muriatic acid</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hydrochloric acid does dissolve efflorescence, but it is easy to overdo on concrete pavers. Too strong a mix etches the surface, exposes the aggregate and can shift the colour permanently. Purpose-made efflorescence cleaners do the same job more gently and are far more forgiving of an imperfect dilution.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blasting sand out of the joints</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A narrow pressure-washer tip held close to the pavers strips joint sand in seconds, and pavers without sand shift and let weeds in. Let the cleaner do the work, use a wide fan tip or a surface cleaner at a shallow angle, and plan to top up the joints afterwards.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing too soon after cleaning</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pavers that feel dry on top can still hold water in their pores after a heavy rinse. Sealer applied over that moisture traps it and often turns milky. Allow at least a day or two of dry weather after cleaning, and longer after rain or in cool, humid conditions, before sealing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cleaning efflorescence too early</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                On new pavers, efflorescence often keeps returning for months while the concrete works through its soluble salts. Cleaning after the first bloom can be wasted effort. Wait until the bloom has slowed, typically after a full season of weather, then clean thoroughly and seal once.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best paver cleaner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a general pre-sealing clean of concrete pavers, Techniseal Paver Prep is our top pick because it removes efflorescence and ground-in dirt without muriatic acid. For routine yearly washing, Simple Green Oxy Solve is better value. For specific stains, use a dedicated oil, rust or moss product, since no single cleaner handles everything well.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you remove white residue from pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                First work out which white residue it is. A powdery bloom that fades when wet is efflorescence and needs an efflorescence cleaner. A rough grey film that appeared after jointing is polymeric sand haze and needs a haze remover. A milky finish after sealing is usually trapped moisture under the sealer, which is a separate problem.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use vinegar or bleach to clean pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Vinegar is a weak acid and can lighten light efflorescence, but it is inconsistent and can etch if left on. Bleach kills algae but can lighten coloured pavers and damage nearby plants. Purpose-made paver cleaners give more predictable results for similar money and are safer for the pavers and the surrounding garden.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will pressure washing damage pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can. A narrow jet held close to the surface can erode the paver face and blast sand out of the joints. Use a wide fan tip or a rotary surface cleaner, keep the nozzle at least a foot away, and let a cleaner loosen the dirt so you only need pressure to rinse.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you get oil stains out of pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Blot fresh spills immediately and cover them with an absorbent. For set-in stains, use a poultice-style remover such as Techniseal Oil & Grease Remover, which draws oil out of the pores as it dries to a powder. Old or large stains often need two or three applications, and a sealed surface resists new stains far better.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I clean pavers before sealing them?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Always. Sealer locks in whatever is on the surface, including efflorescence, oil and dirt, and efflorescence under a sealer can turn it cloudy. Spot-treat stains, clean the whole area with a pre-seal cleaner, rinse thoroughly, re-sand the joints and let the pavers dry completely before sealing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should pavers be cleaned?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
